      heading: "Can you switch if the first ED treatment isn't the best fit?",
      body: `<p><strong>Yes - switching ED treatments is common and easy, and it's often how men find their best fit, since the first option tried isn't always the right one.</strong> You are not locked in, and a good provider expects and supports switching.</p><p>What switching can involve:</p><ul><li><strong>Changing the molecule</strong> - if sildenafil didn't suit you, tadalafil, vardenafil or avanafil might work better or cause fewer side effects.</li><li><strong>Adjusting the dose or strength tier</strong> - sometimes the medication is right but the dose needs tuning.</li><li><strong>Changing dosing style</strong> - from on-demand to a low daily dose, or vice versa, to fit your lifestyle.</li><li><strong>Stepping up to a multi-ingredient option</strong> if a single pill underperformed, or stepping to a second-line treatment if oral options fall short.</li><li><strong>Switching providers</strong> if the service, pricing or format isn't right - just confirm cancellation terms first.</li></ul><p>The key is to <strong>use the follow-up</strong> rather than silently giving up. A legitimate provider offers a way to report that a treatment isn't working and to adjust it - that clinical support is part of what you're paying for, and part of what makes a provider one of the "best." Finding your ideal ED treatment is often an iterative process of a couple of adjustments, not a single perfect first choice - which is exactly why flexibility and support matter in a provider.</p>`,
